---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Claim from '../../components/Claim.astro';
const repo = 'https://github.com/nidhi1603/agent-eval-platform';
---

<Base
  title="Agent Eval Platform · Nidhi Rajani"
  description="A case study: an evaluation platform and runtime harness for a tool-using banking agent on tau2-bench. What it measured, what it found, and why the honest answer was 'no gain'."
>
  <article>
    <header class="wrap case-hero hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Case study · agent evaluation</p>
      <h1>Agent Eval Platform</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Can a harness around the same model complete more banking tasks than the benchmark's standard agent? I built the
        machinery to answer that honestly. On this evidence the answer is <strong>not shown</strong>, and the machinery
        found real problems along the way, including one in the benchmark itself.
      </p>
      <div class="kpis">
        <div class="kpi"><span class="v"><Claim k="aep.leak" /></span><span class="l">dev tasks leaking answer data, reported upstream</span></div>
        <div class="kpi"><span class="v"><Claim k="aep.defects" /></span><span class="l">measurement and harness defects found and fixed</span></div>
        <div class="kpi"><span class="v"><Claim k="aep.claims" /></span><span class="l">false "transfer under way" replies, in replay</span></div>
        <div class="kpi"><span class="v"><Claim k="aep.final" /></span><span class="l">tasks completed, harness vs standard: no gain</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="contact">
        <a class="btn primary" href={repo}>Repo</a>
        <a class="btn" href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/REPORT.md`}>Technical report</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/results/">Every result, filterable</a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="narrow">
      <section class="case-section reveal">
        <h2>The question and the setup</h2>
        <p>
          The benchmark is Sierra's τ-Knowledge banking domain in tau2-bench v1.0.1, pinned and checked on every run. A
          simulated customer asks for help. The agent has to find the right procedure in the bank's knowledge base, unlock
          the tools it names, verify the customer and act within policy. Grading is official: the final database state.
        </p>
        <p>
          I held the model fixed (gpt-5-mini) and changed only what sits around it, so any difference is the harness.
          Experiments ran on the development split. The <Claim k="aep.heldout" /> were never run, so they remain a clean
          test.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="case-section reveal">
        <h2>What I built</h2>
        <p>Six layers, each there to make one number trustworthy:</p>
        <ol class="flow" aria-label="System layers, from model calls to review">
          <li>
            <span class="layer">Models</span>
            Agent, simulated customer and embeddings, all called through LiteLLM.
            <span class="chips"><span>gpt-5-mini (agent)</span><span>gpt-5.2 (customer)</span><span>text-embedding-3-large</span></span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="layer">Spend admission control</span>
            Before every call, reserve its worst-case cost; refuse the call if the cap can't cover it; afterwards, settle
            at the billed cost. A model with no recorded price can't be called at all.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="layer">Benchmark, pinned</span>
            tau2-bench's orchestrator, bank tools, database and grader, verified against a fixed commit and task-file hash.
            The agent's shell tool runs in a sandbox I tightened after canary tests found gaps in the default setup.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="layer">Runtime harness</span>
            Components behind separate flags, each with a negative control: replaying all reference solutions kept the
            same reward in <Claim k="aep.controls" />.
            <span class="chips"><span>tool adapter</span><span>checks before actions</span><span>capability search</span><span>verification evidence</span><span>disclosure check</span><span>nudges</span></span>
          </li>
          <li class="hit">
            <span class="layer">Evaluation layer</span>
            Frozen plans with decision rules written before any spend, paired and balanced batches with a resume journal,
            cheap replay probes, and an answer-independence check that replays everything the agent saw against an
            answer-erased copy of the task.
            <span class="note">→ This found the benchmark leak.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="layer">Review</span>
            Blind exports, two independent model readers plus an adjudicator, and verdict functions frozen before the run.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="case-section reveal">
        <h2>What happened</h2>
        <p>
          The components moved the behaviour they targeted. Transfers per conversation fell (<Claim k="aep.transfers" />).
          Capability search made the account-lookup document reachable in far more of the conversations that needed it (<Claim k="aep.reach" />), though reachable is not the same as used. One rewrite of the feedback after a held
          action cut false "transfer under way" replies (<Claim k="aep.claims" />) in a replay test.
        </p>
        <p>
          Completed tasks did not follow. The best comparison came out <Claim k="aep.best" /> completed pairs (standard
          vs harness). That missed the pre-registered
          gate, and its corrected safety audit found <Claim k="aep.violations" />. The final comparison was <Claim k="aep.final" />.
        </p>
        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Why I didn't call 8 of 22 a win.</strong> At one conversation per task, an identical copy of the agent
          comes out ahead <Claim k="aep.noise" /> of the time. Without a gate frozen before looking, that result becomes a
          "60% improvement" slide.
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="case-section reveal">
        <h2>What the evaluation layer caught</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>A leak in the benchmark.</strong> One listing tool exposed reference-derived data in <Claim k="aep.leak" /> dev tasks. I reported it as <Claim k="gh.issue574" />, with a reproducer that needs no
            model calls and a local fix that leaves every grade unchanged.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Bugs in my own pipeline.</strong> Of the <Claim k="aep.defects" /> defects, one was the benchmark's; the
            rest were mine. Two examples: a per-run cost cap enforced on the upper bound silently cut off only the longer
            arm, and a verification rule accepted values the customer was only echoing back.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>A provenance bug you can run.</strong> An intercepted draft was counted as "shown to the customer", so a
            valid verification was refused. <code>make demo-provenance</code> reproduces it and the fix, offline, for $0.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="case-section reveal">
        <h2>Why I stopped, and what I'd do differently</h2>
        <ul class="limits">
          <li>
            <strong>Reach.</strong> The last component could directly affect only <Claim k="aep.reachP004" /> tasks'
            scores. I chose it because it was tractable, not because it mattered. Next time I'd rank ideas by reach first.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Acting, not finding.</strong> In <Claim k="aep.policyrule" /> agreed policy-step failures, the rule and
            the evidence were both already in the agent's context. Retrieval wasn't the only bottleneck.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Power.</strong> One conversation per task can't detect small effects. I'd estimate power before
            building, and use more trials per task.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="muted small">
          Limits: one model, one domain, development tasks only. None of these results is a leaderboard number; the
          official protocol is every task, four times over. Review labels come from blind model readers, not human
          annotators.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
